Interpretation: minR = fewest exact factorial moments whose LP upper bound on
meas(S7) already lies <= cap_k.  That is the degree of the Bonferroni/dual certificate.

====================================================================================================
DUAL certificate at the closing degree for each k (the binding Bonferroni-type bound)
====================================================================================================
  k=8: degree R=4.  meas(S7) <= (1)*S_0 + (-1)*S_1 + (1)*S_2 + (-9/10)*S_3 + (3/5)*S_4
         = 0.35823 <= cap_k=0.38146   [dual y = ['1', '-1', '1', '-9/10', '3/5']]
  k=9: degree R=3.  meas(S7) <= (1)*S_0 + (-13/18)*S_1 + (4/9)*S_2 + (-1/6)*S_3
         = 0.49288 <= cap_k=0.49426   [dual y = ['1', '-13/18', '4/9', '-1/6']]
  k=10: degree R=3.  meas(S7) <= (1)*S_0 + (-13/18)*S_1 + (4/9)*S_2 + (-1/6)*S_3
         = 0.57008 <= cap_k=0.60440   [dual y = ['1', '-13/18', '4/9', '-1/6']]
  k=11: degree R=2.  meas(S7) <= (1)*S_0 + (-1/2)*S_1 + (1/6)*S_2
         = 0.68305 <= cap_k=0.72527   [dual y = ['1', '-1/2', '1/6']]
  k=12: degree R=2.  meas(S7) <= (1)*S_0 + (-1/2)*S_1 + (1/6)*S_2
         = 0.71417 <= cap_k=0.85714   [dual y = ['1', '-1/2', '1/6']]
  k=13: degree R=2.  meas(S7) <= (1)*S_0 + (-1/2)*S_1 + (1/6)*S_2
         = 0.74888 <= cap_k=1.00000   [dual y = ['1', '-1/2', '1/6']]

DONE.
